        "Traupman 118, 380",
        "M-O s.v. skirt, citing the LRL and Goelzer",
        "Traupman 117: miniskirt, castula brevissima"
      ],
      "comment": "Traupman’s skirt (a miniskirt is <span lang=\"la\">castula brevissima</span>, p. 117). Lewis &amp; Short has it as a woman’s petticoat in a fragment of Varro, but Morgan–Owens notes that the reading there is now rejected for <span lang=\"la\">caltula</span>, so the word may never have been Latin."
    },
    {
      "latin": "calthula, calthulae f.",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "a woman’s short undergarment",
      "evidence": [
        "Plaut. Epid. 231 (Lewis &amp; Short: Plaut. Ep. 2.2.47)",
        "M-O s.v. skirt (<span lang=\"la\">calt(h)ula</span>)"
      ],
      "comment": "Lewis &amp; Short calls it a yellow robe, from <span lang=\"la\">caltha</span>, a marigold; Varro (in Nonius) describes a cloth girded below the breasts. Morgan–Owens’s first choice for a skirt."
    },
    {
